1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,700
Reliable automation is a loop, not a final score.

2
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:16,480
PISAMA makes six stages visible: design the controls, run the agent, retain the evidence, diagnose and heal, verify with evaluations, then prevent the same failure from returning.

3
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,300
Stage one is setup.

4
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:32,500
Before PISAMA sees a result, this n8n workflow declares five synthetic teaching cases, two clean and three failed, with expected failure modes written independently.

5
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:37,000
These cases teach the demo. They never enter the verified release claim.

6
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:43,200
Stage two is execution. I start the workflow in n8n.

7
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:48,200
One branch evaluates each trace. The other retains a stable case identity for review.

8
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:58,350
The workflow itself stays visible, so the evaluation is part of the operating system, not a separate spreadsheet.

9
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:05,100
Stage three is evidence. Clean cases return empty failure-mode sets.

10
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:08,200
The timeout returns F thirteen.

11
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:15,100
The missing-field case returns the contract and expression modes, and the node error is separate.

12
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:18,300
Expected and actual sets match.

13
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:24,600
On a repeat run, every receipt is deduplicated and keeps the original execution identity.

14
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:32,100
Stage four is diagnose and heal. PISAMA separates the failure classes, then keeps the evidence that supports each one.

15
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:40,100
The timeout shows sixty-four seconds against a thirty-second limit, the Slow Code node, and the trace behind F thirteen.

16
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:46,300
The contract finding preserves the missing field and failing expression.

17
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:54,470
Healing begins with an informed human verdict. It does not begin with an automatic mutation.

18
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:02,300
Stage five is verification. Nineteen provenance-backed cases are reviewed and frozen.

19
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:06,000
Eighteen enter the normal regression run. The protected holdout stays excluded.

20
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:14,500
A new asynchronous run succeeds at one hundred percent exact-set accuracy, with its build revision and per-case results stored immutably.

21
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:23,870
If a label changes, PISAMA appends evidence. Earlier scores do not move.

22
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:33,100
Stage six is prevention. For a proven data-contract failure, PISAMA proposes a deterministic input-schema guard.

23
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:39,000
The operator chooses where rejected input goes before apply is allowed.

24
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:48,100
Two real executions verify the result: malformed input is rejected before the consumer, and valid input still passes.

25
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:54,160
That is evidence of recurrence prevention, with rollback retained.

26
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:00,100
The loop is now closed.

27
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:03,300
A run creates evidence. Evidence creates a reviewed diagnosis.

28
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:09,300
The diagnosis changes a control. Evaluations verify the next build, and a real guard proves the failure stays out.

29
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:15,430
That is the PISAMA closed reliability loop.
